## What Makes Short Coffin Nails Different From Every Other Short Shape

Three millimetres of free edge. That is all that separates a short coffin from a short square, and yet the visual difference on the hand is significant.

The coffin shape tapers. From the widest point at the base of the nail, the sides file inward at an angle before the tip is cut flat. That inward taper is the entire personality of the shape. It creates a slimming vertical line along each finger, produces a tip that is narrower than the nail bed it sits on, and gives nail art a flat, architectural platform that rounded shapes simply do not offer. A short square has straight sides with a blunt flat tip. A short round softens everything. A short coffin structures the nail.

Coffin nails are also called ballerina nails, a name that refers to the resemblance to a pointe shoe slipper when viewed from above. At shorter lengths, "short ballerina nails" is the term you will see used interchangeably, particularly on TikTok and in press-on nail descriptions. The shape is identical regardless of which name the brand or nail tech uses.

What makes <strong>short coffin nails</strong> distinct from other short shapes is not just geometry. It is intent. This is a shape that looks deliberate at shorter lengths in a way that square or squoval can feel accidental. The taper signals that a choice was made.

