## Short Coffin vs Squoval: How to Tell the Difference and Which to Choose

Both shapes have a flat tip. That is where the similarity ends, and the confusion begins.

Squoval nails have straight sides from base to tip, with the corners of that square tip very slightly softened or rounded. The nail plate is essentially its full natural width all the way to the free edge. The overall impression is broad and sturdy, practical and flattering on many people, but flat across its visual width.

Short coffin nails taper. The sides file inward before the tip is cut flat, so the free edge is narrower than the nail plate at its widest point. That inward angle is the entire visual difference in a photograph, and it is the reason the two shapes produce such different effects on the finger.

In practice, "short coffin or squoval" is often not a question about aesthetics alone. It is a question about your nail plate shape and what it can support. Very wide nail beds sometimes shape naturally toward squoval because there is enough width to absorb the taper without the sidewalls becoming structurally thin. Narrow nail beds, as covered above, sometimes shape toward squoval for the opposite reason: there isn't enough width for a short taper to visibly narrow. For a full shape decision framework with side-by-side visual logic, the <a href='/blog/coffin-nails/coffin-nails-vs-almond-nails'>Coffin Nails vs Almond Nails</a> comparison addresses the suitability question for every common hand type.

The simplest test: if your nails look wide and flat even after filing, and the taper angle is not registering clearly, you are sitting in squoval territory. If the sides are visibly angling inward toward a narrower flat tip, that is a <strong>short coffin nail</strong>. The difference on the hand can be as small as 1-2mm of taper angle, but the visual effect is not small.

