## How to Maintain the Coffin Shape at Shorter Lengths

One nail file. One habit. Two minutes every ten days.

The challenge with maintaining <strong>short coffin nails</strong> is not the filing, it is the rate of nail growth. As the free edge extends, the taper angle softens and the tip widens. Without maintenance, a short coffin drifts toward squoval within two to three weeks. Catching it early, before significant regrowth, keeps the filing manageable.

The technique: hold your file parallel to the sidewall of the nail, angle it slightly inward, and file in smooth upward strokes rather than a sawing motion. Work one side at a time, stepping back frequently to check the taper is symmetrical. Once both sides are tapered, file the tip horizontally to keep it flat and squared. You are not removing significant length, just restoring the angle of the sides and the flatness of the tip.

For gel extensions, the taper needs to be reworked at the fill appointment. Most nail techs doing a coffin shape will address this automatically; if yours does not, it is worth specifying "can you re-taper the sides" at the start of the fill. The <a href='/blog/coffin-nails/how-to-shape-coffin-nails'>How to Shape Coffin Nails at Home</a> guide covers the step-by-step filing process, including the filing angle and what to do if one side grows asymmetrically.

On natural nails with a gel overlay, the overlay will need a fresh application every three to four weeks. The overlay maintains structural integrity at the sidewalls, without it, the tapered edges on natural nails can be the first point of splitting or chipping.

